-- title:
--   (20), p. 6226 — conditional variance given the public statistic
-- statement:
--   Let $x$ be any stock portfolio, and suppose both indexers and nonindexers are present. Under the Gaussian signal model, the conditional variance of its payoff given the public statistic $y$ is almost surely the constant
--   $$
--   \operatorname{var}(x'v\mid y)
--   =x'\bigl(\Sigma_{vv}-\Sigma_{vy}\Sigma_{yy}(|NI|)^{-1}\Sigma_{yv}\bigr)x.
--   $$
--   This is the portfolio form of equation (20). It connects the actual conditional variance with the covariance matrices of (7)–(8), making those formulas usable for the comparative statics.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The paper's first equality in (20), conditioning on prices instead of $y$, uses (16) and is outside this mission. Here conditioning is on the σ-algebra generated by $y$. The hypothesis $1\le |NI|<m$ keeps $\Sigma_{yy}$ nonsingular; $\Sigma_{vv}$ and $\Sigma_{\varepsilon\varepsilon}$ are positive definite in the model.
-- source:
--   Baruch & Zhang, The Distortion in Prices Due to Passive Investing, Management Science 68(8), 2022, p. 6226, equation (20), with the conditioning equivalence (16) on p. 6224; https://doi.org/10.1287/mnsc.2021.4114

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_PassiveIndexing_PriceEfficiency_Model

namespace PassiveIndexing.PriceEfficiency

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Matrix

/-- Equation (20), p. 6226, applied to `x′v`: conditional variance given the statistic `y`
equals the Gaussian Schur-complement formula. Conditioning on prices is identified with
conditioning on `y` by the paper's (16), which is outside this mission. -/
theorem eq_20 {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] {n m : ℕ}
    (P : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure P]
    (muv : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n))
    (Svv See : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ)
    (v : Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n))
    (eps : Fin m → Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n))
    (hmodel : SignalModel P muv Svv See v eps)
    (NI : Finset (Fin m)) (hNI : Admissible NI)
    (x : Fin n → ℝ) :
    cv P Svv See v eps x NI =ᵐ[P]
      fun _ => condVarFormula m Svv See x (NI.card : ℝ) := by sorry

end PassiveIndexing.PriceEfficiency
